„¥verse¯paragraph_startÃ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ¼Verily I say unto you, Among them that are born of women there hath not risen a greater than John the Baptist: notwithstanding he that is least in the kingdom of heaven is greater than he.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ{And from the days of John the Baptist until now the kingdom of heaven suffereth violence, and the violent take it by force.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ7For all the prophets and the law prophesied until John.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ@And if ye will receive it, this is Elias, which was for to come.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ(He that hath ears to hear, let him hear.¦layout ªlast_verse…¤book'§chapter«first_verseªlast_verse¦verses”„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ}But whereunto shall I liken this generation? It is like unto children sitting in the markets, and calling unto their fellows,¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙoand saying, We have piped unto you, and ye have not danced; we have mourned unto you, and ye have not lamented.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙIFor John came neither eating nor drinking, and they say, He hath a devil.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ­The Son of man came eating and drinking, and they say, Behold a man gluttonous, and a winebibber, a friend of publicans and sinners. But wisdom is justified of her children.¦layout ªlast_verse…¤book'§chapter«first_verseªlast_verse¦verses•„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙjThen began he to upbraid the cities wherein most of his mighty works were done, because they repented not:¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ¾Woe unto thee, Chorazin! woe unto thee, Bethsaida! for if the mighty works, which were done in you, had been done in Tyre and Sidon, they would have repented long ago in sackcloth and ashes.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙgBut I say unto you, It shall be more tolerable for Tyre and Sidon at the day of judgment, than for you.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙÈAnd thou, Capernaum, which art exalted unto heaven, shalt be brought down to hell: for if the mighty works, which have been done in thee, had been done in Sodom, it would have remained until this day.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙpBut I say unto you, That it shall be more tolerable for the land of Sodom in the day of judgment, than for thee.¦layout ªlast_verse…¤book'§chapter«first_verseªlast_verse¦verses“„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ¸At that time Jesus answered and said, I thank thee, O Father, Lord of heaven and earth, because thou hast hid these things from the wise and prudent, and hast revealed them unto babes.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ4Even so, Father: for so it seemed good in thy sight.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙºAll things are delivered unto me of my Father: and no man knoweth the Son, but the Father; neither knoweth any man the Father, save the Son, and he to whomsoever the Son will reveal him.¦layout ªlast_verse…¤book'§chapter«first_verseªlast_verse¦verses“„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙOCome unto me, all ye that labour and are heavy laden, and I will give you rest.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙqTake my yoke upon you, and learn of me; for I am meek and lowly in heart: and ye shall find rest unto your souls.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ,For my yoke is easy, and my burden is light.¦layout ªlast_verse